## SUNDIALS — BSD-3-Clause

Everything under `crates/` is a line-by-line translation of SUNDIALS 7.8.0
(https://github.com/LLNL/sundials), Copyright (c) Lawrence Livermore National
Security and the SUNDIALS contributors. The full text is in `LICENSE`, copied
verbatim from the upstream tree.

Structure, names, constants, algorithms, control flow and message text are
reproduced deliberately — fidelity to the C is the point of the port — so the
translated sources are covered by the SUNDIALS licence and its attribution
requirements travel with them.

## ARM optimized-routines `pow` — MIT

`crates/sundials_core/src/sundials_math.rs` contains a pure-Rust port of the
double-precision `pow` from ARM optimized-routines, taken via musl's
`src/math/pow.c`, `pow_data.c` and `exp_data.c`:

    Copyright (c) 2018, Arm Limited.
    SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

It replaces the platform `f64::powf` because the reference outputs this port is
verified against were generated against glibc >= 2.28, which uses that same
algorithm. Platform libm implementations disagree with it by one ulp on rare
arguments, and inside the step-size heuristics a one-ulp difference forks the
whole trajectory: substituting this implementation turned three examples from
failing to byte-identical. See `VERIFICATION.md`.

The attribution and algorithm provenance are also recorded in a block comment
at the implementation site.

**Scope of this substitution — important.** The ported routine is ordinary
host-independent Rust; despite the "ARM optimized-routines" provenance it is
not an Apple-Silicon-specific or arm64-specific routine. On this project's
target it is not even a substitution for a foreign implementation: that same
algorithm is what glibc >= 2.28 ships as `sysdeps/ieee754/dbl-64/e_pow.c`,
and on x86-64 glibc dispatches to `__ieee754_pow_fma`, the same source built
with `-mfma -mavx2 -ffp-contract=fast`. The Rust routine is measured
bit-exact against that native `pow` — 0 mismatches over 25.9 million inputs
(`tools/pow_differential.sh`, `POW_FMA_EXACTNESS.md` §0).

It was, for a time, the *only* host libm routine this port replaced — every
other transcendental still came from the host, and that residual dependence
held the Windows gate at 125 of 199 variants byte-identical. It no longer
does: `sundials_libm` below replaces `sin`, `cos`, `exp`, `ln`, `asin`,
`acos`, `atan`, `sinh`, `cosh`, `acosh`, `expm1` and `log1p` as well, and the
gate now reads 153 / 26 / 20. (`sqrt`, `mul_add`, `ceil`, `round`, `abs` and
`copysign` remain host calls and are meant to: IEEE-754 specifies them and
they are identical everywhere.) See `README.md` § "Platform scope" and
`current_status.md` §2.

## Deterministic libm — MIT and LGPL-2.1-or-later

`crates/sundials_core/src/sundials_libm/` replaces the host libm for every
transcendental the library and the examples evaluate. It exists because the
upstream SUNDIALS reference `.out` files were generated on a glibc host,
while Rust's `f64::sin`, `f64::exp`, ... are documented as having
*unspecified precision* and forward to whatever libm the host provides — on
this port's target, the Microsoft UCRT, which `tools/libm_fingerprint_win.sh`
shows disagrees with glibc on every one of them. `pow` was taken off the host
first (above); these ten follow the same reasoning.

Two provenances, deliberately kept apart:

**MIT** — `exp.rs`, `log.rs` are the ARM optimized-routines implementations,
translated from musl's `src/math/exp.c`, `exp_data.c`, `log.c`,
`log_data.c`:

    Copyright (c) 2018, Arm Limited.
    SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

glibc >= 2.28 ships the same algorithm as `sysdeps/ieee754/dbl-64/e_exp.c`
and `e_log.c`; musl's copy is used so that these two stay MIT, as `pow` is.

**LGPL-2.1-or-later** — `expm1.rs`, `log1p.rs`, `sincos.rs`, `atan.rs`,
`asincos.rs`, `hyperbolic.rs` are translated from glibc 2.39:

    sysdeps/ieee754/dbl-64/{s_expm1.c, s_log1p.c, s_sin.c, sincostab.c,
      branred.c, usncs.h, s_atan.c, atnat.h, uatan.tbl, e_asin.c, uasncs.h,
      asincos.tbl, root.tbl, powtwo.tbl, e_sinh.c, e_cosh.c, e_acosh.c,
      mydefs.h, dla.h}

    GNU C Library, Copyright (C) The Free Software Foundation, Inc.
    Portions: IBM Accurate Mathematical Library, Copyright (C) International
    Business Machines Corp.
    SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later

A translation is a derivative work, so those six modules are LGPL-2.1+ and
each carries that SPDX header. The lookup tables in particular are copied
data, not independently derived: bit-exactness with glibc is the entire
requirement, so there is no way to reach it without them.

The upstream C is **not** committed to this repository, so the tree itself
carries no LGPL sources; `tools/fetch_libm_sources.sh` downloads it into
`reference/` (gitignored) on demand, pinned to `glibc-2.39`. That keeps the
licence boundary legible, but it does not change the position above: the
Rust translations are here and they are LGPL.

Each routine is measured bit-identical against a real glibc oracle over
4,000,000 inputs per function by `tools/libm_differential_win.sh`; see
`current_status.md`.

## glibc `rand()` — behaviourally reproduced

`crates/arkode_rs/examples/ark_analytic_lsrk_domeigest.rs`,
`ark_brusselator_lsrk_domeigest.rs` and
`ark_brusselator_lsrk_externaldomeigest.rs` each contain a ~40-line Rust
implementation of the classic BSD/glibc `rand()` TYPE_3 additive-feedback
generator (DEG 31, SEP 3, Lehmer/Schrage seeding, 310 discarded outputs,
result = state word >> 1). No glibc source is reproduced — the code is Rust
written against that algorithm description, and the block comment at each site
records it.

It exists for the same reason as the `pow` port: those examples feed
pseudo-random vectors to a dominant-eigenvalue estimator, so the generator's
sequence is output-observable, and the reference `.out` files were produced
against glibc's. Like the `pow` port, it is host-independent Rust. It is
mentioned here so that "only `pow` was taken off the host C library" is read
as the statement it is — a statement about *libm*, not about the whole C
runtime.
